- op: discuss  # 3a58
  at: '2026-09-10T22:09:55Z'  # 3a58
  by: agent  # 3a58
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3a58
  clock: 3  # 3a58
  nonce: 3a58  # 3a58
  message: "GATING QUESTION ANSWERED — and the answer is NO. This item asks, before any code:\n\"Whether the taint engine credits a callback parameter from a registration edge\ndecides whether this buys a FINDING or only a named site in the disclosure. If\nonly the latter, say so on the item and stop.\"\n\n**IT BUYS ONLY A NAMED SITE. The taint engine does NOT credit a callback\nparameter from a registration edge**, and the proof does not depend on building\nanything: `addEventListener` ALREADY reaches the catalogue as net_recv (#753 /\nINV-misup), and it already buys zero findings.\n\nMEASURED 2026-09-10. Record: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/dosuh_js_09102026/\nFixture with THREE arms and a sink the claims actually cover:\n\n  control()                    process.openStdin() -> exec(...)      one function\n  viaAddEventListener()        ws.addEventListener('message',\n                                 ev => exec('ls ' + ev.data))        THE QUESTION\n  insideAnArrowButNotAParam()  same arrow shape, but the value comes\n                                 from openStdin() instead of the param\n\nio-boundaries sees ALL THREE correctly — net_recv 2 chains on\n`WebSocket.addEventListener`, ipc_recv 2 on `process.openStdin`, net_send/subprocess\non the sinks — and the callback is its own symbol\n(`_cb_addEventListener@33:function`). So this is NOT a classification gap.\n\nverify-claims, `untrusted-input-no-subprocess`: **violated, evidence_count 2,\nflow_origins {\"ipc_recv\": 2}. ZERO from net_recv.** Both CONTROLS fire, including\nthe one INSIDE the arrow function. The callback-PARAMETER arm produces nothing.\n\nTHE MECHANISM, read in the code rather than inferred. The analyzer emits\n`references` from the enclosing function to the callback:\n\n    references  viaAddEventListener:function -> _cb_addEventListener@33:function\n    calls       _cb_addEventListener@33:function -> child_process:exec\n\n`TAINT_CALL_EDGE_TYPES` is exactly {calls, dispatches_to, instantiates,\nmodule_attr_ref}. **`references` is not in it**, so by LIVE rule L5 (the walk\nadvances only along VALUE-CARRYING edges) the walk cannot enter the handler at\nall. The net_recv source is attributed to the ENCLOSING function; the sink is\ninside the callback; nothing value-carrying joins them.\n\n**SO THIS IS NOT ABOUT `onmessage =` AND IT IS NOT ABOUT THREE ROWS.** Every\ncallback-registration source in javascript is inert for taint today, including\nthe spelling that already classifies. Implementing this item as filed would add\n`WebSocket.onmessage` to the set of correctly-classified, taint-inert sites — a\nreal disclosure gain (three CONSTRUCT_BLIND_ROWS retired, a declared blindness\nremoved) and, by construction, zero findings.\n\nTHE FIX THAT WOULD BUY FINDINGS IS A DIFFERENT ONE, AND THE SIBLING LANGUAGE\nALREADY HAS IT. python's `argparse_dispatch` emits `dispatches_to` from the\nENCLOSING FUNCTION of a `set_defaults(func=cmd_x)` registration to `cmd_x`, and\nINV-zuhig put `dispatches_to` in TAINT_CALL_EDGE_TYPES precisely because \"a\nframework dispatcher really does hand data to the handler it invokes\" —\n\"without it every framework-dispatched handler was unmintable and 18 self-proof\nconfirms were vacuous\". javascript's callback registrations emit `references`\nwhere python's emit `dispatches_to`. Filed as a separate item.\n\nWHAT I HAVE NOT DECIDED. The \"and stop\" instruction predates the owner's\n2026-09-10 drain ruling (c), under which a T0b cell drains on CORRECTNESS with NO\nprimitive delta required — which removes the premise the stop condition rested\non. I am not treating that as licence to build unilaterally, because the item's\nauthor wrote the condition deliberately. Both readings are on the record; the\nscoped fix is correct-but-inert either way."  # 3a58
