- op: discuss  # 3f08
  at: '2026-09-10T22:25:57Z'  # 3f08
  by: agent  # 3f08
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3f08
  clock: 5  # 3f08
  nonce: 3f08  # 3f08
  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-09-10 — BUILD IT. Option (a). The scoped fix as this item\ndescribes it goes ahead.\n\nWHAT THE COLLISION WAS. This item carries a stop condition written 2026-09-06:\n\"if it buys only a named site in the disclosure, say so and stop.\" Today's\nmeasurement fired it — taint does NOT credit a callback parameter from a\nregistration edge (see the previous entry). But the owner's 2026-09-10 drain\nruling (c) says a T0b cell drains on CORRECTNESS with no primitive delta\nrequired, which removes the premise the stop condition rested on. Two rulings\ncollided; the owner was asked and chose (a).\n\nSO THE STOP CONDITION IS DISCHARGED, NOT IGNORED. Its question was answered\nbefore any code was written, which is what it was for, and the answer is\nrecorded above. What it no longer does is block the build.\n\nBUILD IT KNOWING IT IS CORRECT-BUT-INERT BY DESIGN. This fix will produce ZERO\nsecurity findings and that is the expected outcome, not a regression. Do NOT go\nlooking for findings to justify it and do NOT tag it `awaits_bakeoff_validation`\non a findings claim it cannot make. What it buys:\n  - `ws.onmessage = h` classifies as net_recv instead of emitting nothing;\n  - three rows become reachable — WebSocket.onmessage, WebSocket.onclose,\n    EventSource.onmessage;\n  - the `analyzer_disclosure.CONSTRUCT_BLIND_ROWS[\"javascript\"]` entry is\n    REMOVED, retiring a blindness the tool currently declares in writing.\n\nIT IS ALSO THE PREREQUISITE HALF, which is the part that makes it not throwaway.\n`onmessage =` can never produce a finding unless BOTH this (classification) and\nWI-nisud (the `dispatches_to` edge that lets the walk enter the handler) land.\n`addEventListener` already has this half. Neither item subsumes the other.\n\nWHAT THE FILING ALREADY SPECIFIES, so re-derive none of it:\n  - shape: an `assignment_expression` whose left is a `member_expression` on a\n    receiver the INV-misup path can type (a `new`-constructed catalogued global)\n    and whose property is one the catalogue rows; emit to\n    `<Module>:0-0:<prop>:unresolved` stamped `call_construct: assignment`.\n  - the catalogue side ALREADY matches that construct — WI-zumoz's 2026-08-28\n    probe fed `classify_call` an \"assignment\" construct and all three rows\n    matched. Do not change the catalogue.\n  - TWO PINS FAIL OTHERWISE and must be updated in the SAME change:\n    `test_js_ts_instance_method_calls.py::TestHandlerAssignmentEmitsNoEdge` and\n    `test_construct_blind_disclosure.py::TestTheDeclarationMatchesTheAnalyzer`.\n    Both currently assert the OLD behaviour; overturn them deliberately, with\n    the rationale in the docstring, rather than deleting them.\n\nPROBE THE FAMILY FIRST — it has found more than the filing named on all four\ncells shipped today. Before writing the emitter, enumerate which catalogued\nreceivers have assignable handler properties and which spellings reach them, the\nway the process/performance enumeration was done for WI-kikar. The filing names\nthree rows; that is the floor, not the ceiling."  # 3f08
