- op: discuss  # ad16
  at: '2026-09-10T23:58:18Z'  # ad16
  by: agent  # ad16
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ad16
  clock: 7  # ad16
  nonce: ad16  # ad16
  message: "DONE — merged as PR #903, dev 804a402089.\n\nBEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE (live repro on the production path, not a proxy metric).\nFixtures and outputs: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/dosuh_family_09102026/.\n\n  $ hypergumbo survey <fixture> --out hg.json\n  $ hypergumbo io-boundaries --input hg.json --format json\n  total_io_edges: 6\n    net_recv {'WebSocket.addEventListener': 1, 'WebSocket.onmessage': 1}\n\n`WebSocket.onmessage` appears as a net_recv chain for the first time, beside\nthe addEventListener spelling on the same receiver. Before this change the\nassignment emitted no edge of any kind.\n\nTHE FILING NAMED ONE RECEIVER SPELLING; THE PROBE FOUND FOUR. Each spelling was\nasked TWICE, once as `ws.send(x)` and once as `ws.onmessage = h`, so scope was\nset by parity with the call path rather than by the construct:\n\n  const ws = new WebSocket(u)      CALL: tagged   ASSIGN: nothing  -> this fix\n  function f(ws: WebSocket)        CALL: tagged   ASSIGN: nothing  -> this fix\n  new WebSocket(u).onmessage = h   CALL: tagged   ASSIGN: nothing  -> this fix\n  module scope                     CALL: tagged   ASSIGN: nothing  -> this fix\n  let ws; ws = new WebSocket(u)    CALL: external ASSIGN: nothing  -> WI-ponid\n  this.ws = new WebSocket(u)       CALL: external ASSIGN: nothing  -> WI-ponid\n  ws['send'](x)                    CALL: nothing  ASSIGN: nothing  -> WI-vipos\n\nThe three that resolve for NEITHER are receiver-typing gaps predating this\nconstruct; fixing them here would have meant a second copy of receiver\nresolution, so they are filed and pinned as known gaps.\n\nTHE INERTNESS PREDICTION IN THE OWNER'S BRIEF IS REFUTED. The brief said this\nwould buy zero findings and instructed me not to hunt for any. I did not hunt —\nI ran verify-claims to CONFIRM inertness and got the opposite:\n\n  untrusted-input-no-subprocess  violated  evidence_count=1  origins={'net_recv': 1}\n    WebSocket.onmessage [app.js:viaAssignment] -> child_process.exec\n\nIt is a TRUE finding: `ws.onmessage = function (ev) { exec('ls ' + ev.data); }`\nreally does carry network bytes into a shell.\n\nWHY THE PREDICTION FAILED, ATTRIBUTED BEFORE BEING BELIEVED. addEventListener\npasses its callback as an ARGUMENT, and WI-zavad mints that callback its own\nsymbol (`_cb_addEventListener@33`); the sink hangs off THAT symbol behind a\n`references` edge the taint walk cannot cross — inert, as predicted. An\nassignment RHS is minted NO symbol, so an inline handler's body is attributed to\nthe enclosing function and source and sink land on one node. Verified by reading\nthe edges, not inferred.\n\nSO THE THREE SPELLINGS NOW DIFFER:\n  inline handler, sink in body   -> FINDING (true positive)\n  addEventListener, same program -> none (callback symbol + references edge)\n  named handler (`= handle`)     -> none, as predicted; and there is no edge of\n                                    ANY type from the registrant to `handle`\n                                    (filed as WI-vubal)\n\nNOT TAGGED awaits_bakeoff_validation, and the reason is not the brief. No\ncorpus-level quantitative claim was made — the evidence here is fixture-level.\nThe corpus-level finding delta is genuinely UNMEASURED and is filed separately\nrather than asserted either way.\n\nFALSE-POSITIVE SHAPE CHECKED AND NOT INTRODUCED HERE. A probe with a harmless\nhandler and an unrelated sibling `exec` produces a finding — but it produces one\nfor addEventListener identically. That is the structural arm's pre-existing\nco-location imprecision, not something this construct adds.\n\nDELIBERATELY NOT DONE: minting a symbol for the assignment RHS. It would make\nthe inline spelling inert too, reproducing exactly the defect WI-nisud exists to\nremove and trading a true finding for symmetry. The collapse is accidentally\nCORRECT — the handler body genuinely executes with the received value in scope.\nThe structure is pinned by a test so it cannot change silently.\n\nALSO IN THIS CHANGE: CONSTRUCT_BLIND_ROWS' javascript entry removed (the dict\nships empty, machinery kept and tested against an injected declaration); two\npins overturned with rationale rather than deleted; the spec's ADR-0049\nparagraph and test_inv_nular_false_sources.py's hold docstring re-pointed, since\nboth rested on these rows being unreachable and the latter named this exact\ntrigger. The rows were NOT moved — that is WI-vogos."  # ad16
